WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.888
Welcome to CSC3100

00:00:02.888 --> 00:00:04.110
Data Structures.

00:00:04.182 --> 00:00:07.129
Analysing a data structure means counting

00:00:07.201 --> 00:00:09.501
how much work its operations do

00:00:09.573 --> 00:00:12.017
and those counts are almost always

00:00:12.089 --> 00:00:14.533
written with three things: powers

00:00:14.533 --> 00:00:16.114
logarithms, and sums.

00:00:16.186 --> 00:00:18.559
This first part reviews the facts

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:20.140
we will use all term.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:21.759
Here is a taste

00:00:21.838 --> 00:00:24.376
a million elements can be halved

00:00:24.456 --> 00:00:26.359
only about twenty times.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:29.770
By the end of this part, you will see why.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:32.284
Start with exponents.

00:00:32.377 --> 00:00:35.140
Multiplying powers of the same

00:00:35.232 --> 00:00:37.443
base adds the exponents

00:00:37.443 --> 00:00:39.746
dividing subtracts them

00:00:39.746 --> 00:00:43.430
and a power of a power multiplies them.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.900
Two consequences are easy to get wrong.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:50.340
Take 2^N, and 2^N again.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.820
Adding them doubles the value

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:54.900
put the two side by side

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.460
and you get 2 * 2^N

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.020
which is 2^(N+1).

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:02.660
It does not double the exponent

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.300
the sum is not 2^(2N).

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:08.885
Doubling the exponent is what squaring does

00:01:08.958 --> 00:01:10.796
X^N * X^N

00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:12.340
is X^(2N).

00:01:12.413 --> 00:01:14.325
A short program checks the

00:01:14.398 --> 00:01:15.942
sum for N=10

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:19.250
both sides are 2048.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.810
A logarithm answers one question

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:26.422
to which power must I raise X to get B?

00:01:26.512 --> 00:01:29.402
X^A = B exactly when

00:01:29.492 --> 00:01:31.750
log_X(B) = A.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.416
In this course, every logarithm is base 2

00:01:35.493 --> 00:01:37.344
unless we say otherwise.

00:01:37.344 --> 00:01:40.352
log(1) is 0, log(2) is 1

00:01:40.352 --> 00:01:43.745
log(1024) is 10

00:01:43.822 --> 00:01:46.521
and log(65536)

00:01:46.598 --> 00:01:49.220
is 16.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:52.244
The change-of-base rule says that

00:01:52.325 --> 00:01:55.241
logarithms in different bases differ

00:01:55.322 --> 00:01:57.429
only by a constant factor

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:00.588
log_2(N) is about 3.32

00:02:00.669 --> 00:02:03.424
times log_10(N).

00:02:03.424 --> 00:02:05.530
And the usual rules hold

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:09.176
the log of a product is the sum of the logs

00:02:09.176 --> 00:02:12.416
the log of A^B is B * log(A).

00:02:12.416 --> 00:02:15.090
And log x is always less than x.

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:17.961
Here is why logarithms appear

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.094
everywhere in this course.

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:23.253
The floor of log2(n) is the number

00:02:23.332 --> 00:02:26.492
of times you can halve n, rounding down

00:02:26.492 --> 00:02:28.230
before you reach 1.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:30.718
Java has no log2 method,

00:02:30.797 --> 00:02:33.331
so the program in the notes also

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.706
counts the halvings directly.

00:02:35.706 --> 00:02:38.002
While n is greater than 1,

00:02:38.002 --> 00:02:41.248
divide it by 2, with integer division,

00:02:41.248 --> 00:02:42.119
and count.

00:02:42.119 --> 00:02:43.940
Then return the count.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:47.528
For 1, 2, 1024

00:02:47.603 --> 00:02:49.711
and 65536,

00:02:49.786 --> 00:02:51.518


00:02:51.518 --> 00:02:53.777
the count is exactly log2.

00:02:53.777 --> 00:02:54.906
For 1,000,000,

00:02:54.906 --> 00:02:58.069
log2 is 19.932,

00:02:58.069 --> 00:03:00.930
and the count is 19: the floor.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:02.378
Now you try.

00:03:02.378 --> 00:03:05.486
What does halvings(1000) return?

00:03:05.559 --> 00:03:07.583
And halvings(1023)?

00:03:07.655 --> 00:03:08.884


00:03:08.884 --> 00:03:11.270
Pause the video and work it out.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:16.938
Both return 9.

00:03:16.938 --> 00:03:21.115
Watch 1000: 500,

00:03:21.115 --> 00:03:23.940
250,

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:28.854
125, 62,

00:03:28.854 --> 00:03:33.767
31, 15, 7, 3, 1.

00:03:33.767 --> 00:03:35.610
9 halvings.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:38.858
2^9 is 512,

00:03:38.858 --> 00:03:40.947
which is at most 1000.

00:03:41.015 --> 00:03:42.632
And 1000 is less

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:44.047
than 2^10

00:03:44.047 --> 00:03:46.069
(1024).

00:03:46.137 --> 00:03:48.361
So every number from 500

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:51.326
12 to 1023

00:03:51.394 --> 00:03:52.742
takes 9 halvings,

00:03:52.809 --> 00:03:54.966
and 1024

00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:56.920
is the first that takes 10.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:58.186
In general,

00:03:58.186 --> 00:04:01.849
halvings(n) is the floor of log2(n).

00:04:01.932 --> 00:04:04.846
Any algorithm that throws away half

00:04:04.929 --> 00:04:07.177
of its input at every step,

00:04:07.177 --> 00:04:10.923
like binary search, takes about log n steps.

00:04:11.006 --> 00:04:14.086
That is why 1,000,000 elements can be

00:04:14.169 --> 00:04:16.750
halved only about 20 times.

00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:18.381
Next, sums.

00:04:18.381 --> 00:04:22.392
The sum 1 + 2 + 3, up to N

00:04:22.392 --> 00:04:24.397
has a picture proof.

00:04:24.397 --> 00:04:26.689
Write the sum forwards

00:04:26.689 --> 00:04:29.650
and then backwards underneath.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.518
Now add the two rows column by column

00:04:33.518 --> 00:04:36.665
1 + N, 2 + N - 1

00:04:36.665 --> 00:04:38.887
3 + N - 2.

00:04:38.887 --> 00:04:42.219
Every column adds up to N + 1.

00:04:42.219 --> 00:04:44.163
There are N columns

00:04:44.163 --> 00:04:47.866
so twice the sum is N * (N + 1)

00:04:47.866 --> 00:04:51.754
and the sum itself is N * (N + 1)

00:04:51.754 --> 00:04:52.680
/ 2.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:55.988
The sum of the squares is N *

00:04:56.078 --> 00:04:59.843
(N + 1) * (2N + 1) / 6.

00:04:59.843 --> 00:05:01.009
For large N

00:05:01.009 --> 00:05:04.864
the first sum is about N^2 / 2

00:05:04.864 --> 00:05:08.540
and the second about N^3 / 3.

00:05:08.629 --> 00:05:11.588
You will meet the first one every

00:05:11.677 --> 00:05:14.636
time a loop runs 1, 2, 3

00:05:14.636 --> 00:05:18.670
up to N times in turn, as in insertion sort.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:22.750
For example, 2 + 5 + 8

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:25.204
up to 3k - 1.

00:05:25.204 --> 00:05:28.345
Each term is 3i - 1

00:05:28.443 --> 00:05:31.192
so the sum splits into 3

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:34.038
* (sum of i) - k.

00:05:34.038 --> 00:05:38.455
That simplifies to k * (3k + 1)

00:05:38.455 --> 00:05:39.437
/ 2.

00:05:39.437 --> 00:05:42.087
Check with k = 3

00:05:42.087 --> 00:05:45.719
2 + 5 + 8 is 15

00:05:45.719 --> 00:05:49.940
and 3 * 10 / 2 is 15.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:53.106
Now the geometric series

00:05:53.106 --> 00:05:57.760
1 + A + A^2, up to A^N.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:01.173
Call it S, and multiply it by A.

00:06:01.173 --> 00:06:04.276
Subtract: every term cancels

00:06:04.379 --> 00:06:06.965
except the first one of S

00:06:07.069 --> 00:06:09.551
and the last one of A S.

00:06:09.654 --> 00:06:12.447
So S - AS is 1 -

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:14.619
A^(N + 1)

00:06:14.722 --> 00:06:17.308
and dividing by 1 -

00:06:17.411 --> 00:06:19.480
A gives the formula.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:22.484
If A is between 0 and 1

00:06:22.484 --> 00:06:26.327
the term A^(N + 1) is positive

00:06:26.327 --> 00:06:30.354
so the sum is at most 1 / (1 - A)

00:06:30.354 --> 00:06:31.543
for every N.

00:06:31.543 --> 00:06:35.570
Take A = 1/2: 1, plus 1/2

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:38.041
plus 1/4, and so on.

00:06:38.132 --> 00:06:40.328
Each piece fills half of

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:42.342
the gap that is left

00:06:42.342 --> 00:06:45.270
so the total never reaches 2.

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:49.887
This is why halving the work at every step costs

00:06:49.976 --> 00:06:53.264
only a constant times the first step

00:06:53.264 --> 00:06:56.464
n + n/2 + n/4

00:06:56.464 --> 00:06:59.220
and so on, is < 2n.

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:01.640
With A = 2

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:05.987
the formula gives 1 + 2 + 4

00:07:05.987 --> 00:07:08.057
up to 2^N

00:07:08.057 --> 00:07:12.301
= 2^(N+1) - 1

00:07:12.301 --> 00:07:16.130
one less than the next power of 2.

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:20.036
A program in the notes checks these formulas

00:07:20.112 --> 00:07:22.455
by adding the terms one by one.

00:07:22.455 --> 00:07:24.798
1 + 2, up to 100

00:07:24.798 --> 00:07:26.915
is 5050

00:07:26.915 --> 00:07:28.880
just as the formula says.

00:07:28.956 --> 00:07:31.224
The squares give 300

00:07:31.299 --> 00:07:33.265
and 38000

00:07:33.265 --> 00:07:35.155
350.

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:37.574
The partial sums of (1/2)^i

00:07:37.649 --> 00:07:40.900
creep up towards 2: 1.5

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:43.697
1.96875

00:07:43.697 --> 00:07:46.796
1.999999.

00:07:46.871 --> 00:07:50.046
And the powers of 2 up to 2^10

00:07:50.122 --> 00:07:53.070
add up to 2047.

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:55.547
But checking a formula on a

00:07:55.626 --> 00:07:57.674
few values is not a proof.

00:07:57.674 --> 00:07:59.092
It catches typos

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:01.770
only a proof shows that a formula

00:08:01.849 --> 00:08:03.267
holds for every N.

00:08:03.267 --> 00:08:06.260
That is the subject of the next part.

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:08.345
Also watch the types.

00:08:08.345 --> 00:08:11.733
N * (N+1) * (2N+1)

00:08:11.733 --> 00:08:13.303
as an int product

00:08:13.303 --> 00:08:16.773
overflows once N passes about 1000.

00:08:16.855 --> 00:08:19.582
That is why the program keeps the

00:08:19.664 --> 00:08:21.730
sum of squares in a long.

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:23.260
To sum up.

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:23.735
1.

00:08:23.735 --> 00:08:27.820
adding two equal powers doubles the value

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:30.955
it does not double the exponent.

00:08:30.955 --> 00:08:33.615
And log means log base 2.

00:08:33.615 --> 00:08:36.370
2. The floor of log2(n)

00:08:36.465 --> 00:08:38.555
counts the halvings

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:41.215
so 1000000 elements halve

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:43.590
only about 20 times.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:44.255
3.

00:08:44.255 --> 00:08:48.530
the arithmetic series is N * (N+1)

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:49.480
/ 2

00:08:49.575 --> 00:08:53.280
and a geometric series with A < 1

00:08:53.375 --> 00:08:56.510
stays below 1 / (1-A).

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:59.802
Try this without a calculator

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:04.019
1 + 3 + 9 + 27

00:09:04.117 --> 00:09:05.687
+ 81.

00:09:05.785 --> 00:09:09.021
And 1 plus a third plus a ninth

00:09:09.119 --> 00:09:13.140
plus a twenty-seventh, and so on forever.

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:16.425
In part two: how to prove things

00:09:16.425 --> 00:09:20.250
from a single counterexample to induction.

